---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../../components/DBox.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Alerts that triage themselves — Andrew Yang"
  description="An alert is a question, and the answer used to require me at a keyboard. A headless agent now investigates first and sends the conclusion — with three ways to say 'I already know' that can never hide the alert itself."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/lab/`}>← Lab</a></p>
  <span class="tag">operations · AI</span>
  <h1>Alerts that triage themselves</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Once <a href={`${base}/lab/earned-green/`}>every green had to be earned</a>,
    the reds started arriving reliably — which exposed the next problem. An
    alert is not information; it is a question, and answering it meant being at
    a keyboard. So the machine now answers its own questions first, and sends
    me the conclusion instead of the symptom.
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 314"
    alt="Alertmanager fans out to two paths. One goes to my phone's chat channel every single time, unconditionally. The other publishes to a subscription topic which a bridge on my own machine consumes one alert at a time; a known-issues gate is consulted, then a headless agent investigates on the box itself and pushes a written conclusion to my phone. When the gate says the issue is known and unchanged, the investigation still runs but the result is only written to a log."
    caption="Two exits from the same route. Everything clever happens on the right-hand path; the left-hand path is deliberately too dumb to fail."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={24} title="Alertmanager" sub="37 rules on the notify path" />
    <DBox x={335} y={24} title="Subscription topic" sub="outbound only — the machine" sub2="subscribes, nothing is exposed" />
    <DBox x={660} y={24} title="Bridge on my machine" sub="one alert at a time" />

    <DBox x={10} y={134} dashed title="Chat, every time" sub="the human path — nothing" sub2="below can suppress it" />
    <DBox x={335} y={134} title="Headless investigation" sub="read-mostly tools, safe fixes only," sub2="conclusion in the first sentence" />
    <DBox x={660} y={134} accent title="Known-issues gate" sub={'"I am already on this one"'} sub2="— claimed by alert name" />

    <DBox x={335} y={244} title="Push to my phone" sub="the answer, not the symptom" />
    <DBox x={660} y={244} dashed title="Known and unchanged" sub="log only — the phone" sub2="stays quiet" />

    <path class="flow" d="M240,54 L329,54" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M565,54 L654,54" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow dash" d="M125,84 L125,128" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M775,84 L775,128" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M660,164 L571,164" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M450,194 L450,238" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow dash" d="M775,194 L775,238" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
  </Diagram>

  <h2>What the machine does before it interrupts me</h2>
  <p>
    A firing alert publishes to a topic my own machine subscribes to, so the
    investigator needs no inbound port, no tunnel and no exposed service — the
    box reaches out, the way every other piece of this platform does. The
    bridge hands one alert at a time to a headless agent session that reads a
    runbook, the alert payload, and the machine itself: logs, unit status,
    sockets, disk, the three network uplinks. It is allowed to take safe,
    reversible actions — restart a crashed user service, clear a temp
    directory — and explicitly not allowed to take irreversible ones. Anything
    destructive comes back as a recommendation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The output is shaped by its delivery: a phone notification truncated to
    roughly 900 characters. So the runbook demands the conclusion first, then
    what was done, then what I should do. In the first four weeks it handled
    <strong>76 alerts</strong> this way. The ones that mattered arrived as a
    diagnosis with the evidence attached; the rest never woke me up at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Three ways to say "I already know"</h2>
  <p>
    An investigator that re-investigates is worse than none: it trains you to
    swipe the notification away, which is exactly the habit the whole platform
    exists to prevent. So there are three suppressions, and the interesting
    part is that they are deliberately not interchangeable.
  </p>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>Fingerprint de-duplication — "don't burn the same investigation
      twice."</strong> Automatic, time-windowed, invisible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A known-issues file — "I know about this one, stop telling
      me."</strong> Hand-written, claimed per alert name, opt-in per entry. On a
      hit the investigation <em>still runs</em>, because a known problem getting
      worse is the thing I most need to hear about; only an explicit
      "known, nothing new" verdict is downgraded to a log line.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An upstream silence — "both of you be quiet, I'm doing
      maintenance."</strong> Expires on its own.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The invariant that makes this safe to build: <strong>only the third one can
    silence the human channel.</strong> The first two live entirely on the
    automated path, so the worst bug I can write into them costs me one missing
    robot opinion — never a missing alert. That single rule is what let me hand
    a notification budget to a machine at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Two failures worth keeping</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>An off-by-one in the time domain.</strong> The de-duplication window
    was 20 hours; the repeat interval for warning-severity alerts was 24. Every
    critical alert was suppressed correctly, which is why the setting looked
    healthy for three weeks — but every warning came back at 24.1 hours, cleared
    the 20-hour window by a comfortable margin, and was investigated again.
    Three of them did that daily for a week: full investigations, two phone
    pushes each, not one of them caught. A suppression window is not a duration,
    it is a claim about a <em>cycle</em>, and it has to be longer than the
    longest cycle it is supposed to absorb. It is now 25 hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Documentation that reached into production.</strong> The known-issues
    file is prose for me and structured data for the handler, which is a hazard:
    the format example at the top of the file was, syntactically, a perfectly
    valid claim on a real alert name. A worked example in a document should not
    be able to suppress a production alert — the handler now skips fenced
    blocks, and the example uses names that do not exist. Two guards, because
    the failure would have been completely silent.
  </p>

  <h2>Where I drew the line</h2>
  <p>
    The agent investigates and reports; it does not decide what is worth
    knowing. Every alert still reaches me through a path that has no gate on
    it, and the escalation is a human one — when the answer is not enough, I
    open the same session from my phone and keep going with the context already
    loaded. The design goal was never an unattended operator. It was to stop
    paying the fixed cost of "open the laptop, ssh in, read four logs" before I
    even know whether it matters.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'd tell my past self</h2>
  <p>
    Automating the <em>response</em> to alerts is much less dangerous than it
    sounds, provided you never let the automation stand between the alert and
    the human. Put the intelligence on a branch, keep the trunk stupid, and
    every mistake you make in the clever part is recoverable by definition.
  </p>
</Base>
